.TH "zprune" "8" "" "" "System Manager\(cqs Manual"
.SH NAME
\f[B]zprune\f[R] \- destroy snapshot prune candidates selected by zelta
prune
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]zprune\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[I]source\f[R] [\f[I]target\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]zprune\f[R] is the destructive companion to \f[B]zelta prune\f[R].
It runs \f[B]zelta prune\f[R] with the supplied options, previews
reported candidates with \f[B]zfs destroy \-nvp\f[R], asks for
confirmation, and then destroys the same candidates with \f[B]zfs
destroy\f[R].
.PP
Candidate selection belongs to \f[B]zelta prune\f[R].
Destruction belongs to \f[B]zprune\f[R].
This keeps the safety boundary explicit: \f[B]zelta prune\f[R] reports,
\f[B]zprune\f[R] destroys.
.PP
The \f[I]source\f[R] and optional \f[I]target\f[R] operands have the
same meaning as in \f[B]zelta\-prune(8)\f[R].
Remote source destruction is run through \f[B]ZELTA_REMOTE_COMMAND\f[R],
which defaults to \f[B]ssh(1)\f[R].
.SH OPTIONS
.SS zprune Options
.TP
\f[B]\-f\f[R], \f[B]\(enforce\f[R]
Destroy previewed candidates without asking for confirmation.
Candidates are still selected by \f[B]zelta prune\f[R] and previewed
with \f[B]zfs destroy \-nvp\f[R] before destruction.
.TP
\f[B]\-q\f[R], \f[B]\(enquiet\f[R]
Do not display the snapshot candidate list or destroy preview.
If \f[B]\(enforce\f[R] is also used, \f[B]zprune\f[R] suppresses the
prompt and proceeds after validation.
.TP
\f[B]\-n\f[R], \f[B]\(endryrun\f[R], \f[B]\(endry\-run\f[R]
Display compact \f[B]zfs destroy\f[R] commands for selected candidates,
print the summary, and exit without prompting or destroying snapshots.
With \f[B]\(enverbose\f[R], expand snapshot ranges in the command
preview.
With \f[B]\(enquiet\f[R], print only the summary and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\(enhelp\f[R]
Show command usage.
.TP
\f[B]\-V\f[R], \f[B]\(enversion\f[R]
Show \f[B]zprune\f[R] version information.
.SS ZELTA PRUNE OPTIONS
Most options are passed directly to \f[B]zelta prune\f[R].
See \f[B]zelta\-prune(8)\f[R] for complete behavior and safety details.
.PP
Common prune options:
.TP
\f[B]\(enprune\-num\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Keep the newest \f[I]N\f[R] snapshots after the guard point.
.TP
\f[B]\(enprune\-time\f[R] \f[I]TIME\f[R]
Keep snapshots newer than \f[I]TIME\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\(enprune\-grid\f[R] \f[I]GRID\f[R]
Apply GFS\-style grid retention, such as
\f[CR]30x1 day, 52x1 week, 1 year\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\(enprune\-size\f[R] \f[I]SIZE\f[R]
Select oldest eligible snapshots until \f[I]SIZE\f[R] bytes are reached.
.TP
\f[B]\(enprune\-guard\f[R] \f[CR]latest\f[R]|\f[CR]unsynced\f[R]|\f[CR]none\f[R]
Select target safety behavior.
.TP
\f[B]\(enno\-prune\-guard\f[R]
Disable target matching checks.
.TP
\f[B]\(eninclude\f[R] \f[I]PATTERN\f[R]
Include only datasets or snapshots matching \f[I]PATTERN\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-X\f[R], \f[B]\(enexclude\f[R] \f[I]PATTERN\f[R]
Exclude datasets or snapshots matching \f[I]PATTERN\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-d\f[R], \f[B]\(endepth\f[R] \f[I]LEVELS\f[R]
Limit dataset\-tree recursion depth.
.SH SAFETY MODEL
\f[B]zprune\f[R] applies these checks before destruction:
.IP \(bu 2
candidates are selected by \f[B]zelta prune\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
candidates are validated before preview;
.IP \(bu 2
candidates are grouped per dataset and previewed with \f[B]zfs destroy
\-nvp\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
destruction uses the same grouped candidate form as the preview;
.IP \(bu 2
the prompt summarizes snapshot count and estimated reclaimed space;
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\(endryrun\f[R] shows compact destroy commands and summary, then
exits before prompting;
.IP \(bu 2
the operator must answer \f[CR]y\f[R] or \f[CR]yes\f[R] unless
\f[B]\(enforce\f[R] is used;
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]zfs destroy \-R\f[R] is never used.
.PP
Unless \f[B]\(enno\-prune\-guard\f[R] is used, a target operand is
required so \f[B]zelta prune\f[R] can confirm target safety before
\f[B]zprune\f[R] destroys source snapshots.
.PP
If the source is remote, destruction is executed on that source host
through \f[B]ZELTA_REMOTE_COMMAND\f[R].
The default remote command is \f[B]ssh\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
Preview default prune candidates and confirm before destroying:
.IP
.EX
zprune tank/data backup:tank/data
.EE
.PP
Destroy without the confirmation prompt:
.IP
.EX
zprune \-\-force tank/data backup:tank/data
.EE
.PP
Preview destroy commands and summary without prompting:
.IP
.EX
zprune \-\-dryrun tank/data backup:tank/data
.EE
.PP
Print only the dry\-run summary:
.IP
.EX
zprune \-qn tank/data backup:tank/data
.EE
.PP
Use an explicit keep window:
.IP
.EX
zprune \-\-prune\-num=60 \-\-prune\-time=\(aq14 days\(aq \(rs
    tank/data backup:tank/data
.EE
.PP
Apply grid retention:
.IP
.EX
zprune \-\-prune\-grid=\(aq30x1 day, 52x1 week, 1 year\(aq \(rs
    tank/data backup:tank/data
.EE
.PP
Prune from the oldest eligible snapshots until at least 10 GiB is
selected:
.IP
.EX
zprune \-\-prune\-size=10G tank/data backup:tank/data
.EE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]ZELTA_REMOTE_COMMAND\f[R]
Command used for remote source destruction.
The default is \f[CR]ssh\f[R].
.PP
Other \f[B]ZELTA_\f[R] variables used by \f[B]zelta prune\f[R] are
honored.
\f[B]zprune\f[R] bootstraps through \f[B]zelta\f[R], so
\f[B]ZELTA_ENV\f[R] settings such as \f[B]PRUNE_GUARD\f[R] are loaded
before prune candidates are selected.
See \f[B]zelta\-options(7)\f[R] and \f[B]zelta\-prune(8)\f[R].
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success and non\-zero on error, aborted confirmation,
failed candidate preview, or failed destruction.
.SH NOTES
For non\-destructive candidate reporting, use \f[B]zelta prune\f[R]
directly.
To preview the exact destroy operations that \f[B]zprune\f[R] would run,
use \f[B]zprune \(endryrun\f[R].
.PP
Manual piping from \f[B]zelta prune\f[R] to \f[B]zfs destroy\f[R] is
discouraged.
\f[B]zprune\f[R] preserves the review, preview, summary, and
confirmation workflow.
